 ],
 "literal": "Rain clouds resting great mountain's lord, / mastwood flower garland sharp sword Andiran's / hill did they sing? / male elephants abundantly possessing this beauty obtaining forest indeed!",
 "translation": "Is it because they sang of the hill of Andiran, the lord of the great mountain where rain clouds rest, who wears a garland of mastwood flowers and wields a sharp sword, that this beautiful forest has come to possess so many male elephants?",
 "modern": "மழைக் கூட்டங்கள் தங்கும் பெரிய மலைக்குத் தலைவனும், வழை மலர் மாலையையும் கூர்மையான வாளையும் உடையவனுமாகிய அண்டிரனுடைய மலையைப் பாடினார்களோ? ஆண் யானைகளை மிக அதிகமாகக் கொண்டுள்ள இந்த அழகு பொருந்திய காடு!",
 "explanation": "This poem belongs to the Puram genre, specifically the Padan tinai and the theme of Iyanmoli, where the poet Uraiyur Enicceri Mudamosiyar praises the chieftain Ay Andiran. The poet marvels at the sheer number of male elephants roaming the beautiful forest. He wonders aloud if the forest itself, or the bards, sang of the hills of the generous patron Ay Andiran. Because of singing his praise, the forest has been blessed with a wealth of elephants, which are traditional symbols of the chieftain's boundless generosity. The imagery of rain clouds resting on the mountain signifies the chieftain's constant and natural giving, which showers wealth upon those who seek him.",
